---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="ContestLogX – Cross-Platform Ham Radio Contest Logging">
  <div class="flex flex-col items-center px-5 py-10 text-center">

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="max-w-4xl mb-0">
      <!-- Logo section with images and text side by side -->
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-8 mb-6">
        <!-- Left: Images side by side -->
        <div class="flex gap-4 items-center">
          <img src="/radio.png" alt="Radio" style="height: 120px; width: auto;" />
          <img src="/antenna.png" alt="Antenna" style="height: 120px; width: auto;" />
        </div>

        <!-- Right: Text -->
        <div class="text-left">
          <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold mb-2" style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;">ContestLogX</h1>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold opacity-70" style="letter-spacing: 0.1em;">HAM RADIO CONTEST LOGGER</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg opacity-80 mb-0">
        Cross-Platform, fast, clean, modern contest logging for serious operators.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Why section -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mt-10 text-left leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Why another contest logger?</h2>
      <p class="opacity-80 mb-4">
        Most contest loggers are Windows-only, closed-source, or locked to a fixed set of contests.
        ContestLogX is built from the ground up to run natively on Linux, macOS, and Windows — no
        emulation, no workarounds. Every contest is defined in a simple JSON file, so you can add
        support for any contest yourself without touching a line of C++. Whether you're a serious
        DX contester or running a small club-sponsored event, ContestLogX adapts to you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Features -->
    <section class="w-full max-w-3xl mt-2 text-left leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">ContestLogX Features</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li>Cross-Platform: Linux, macOS, Windows — first-class on all three</li>
        <li>User-defined contest modules in JSON — add any contest without recompiling</li>
        <li>Full rig control via <a href="https://www.w1hkj.org/" class="underline" style="color: #58a6ff;">flrig</a></li>
        <li>Lightning‑fast logging optimized for contest workflow</li>
        <li>Clean, modern UI with dark‑theme focus</li>
        <li>Callsign lookup and exchange autofill</li>
        <li>CW and SSB Memories utilizing TTS (text-to-speech)</li>
        <li>Import and export Cabrillo and ADIF formats</li>
        <li>Designed by an active contester for real‑world use</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <br />

    <!-- Beta Invite -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mt-0 px-5 py-8 rounded-lg border" style="background-color: #161b22; border-color: #30363d;">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-0">Linux Beta Program</h2>
      <p>
        We're looking for experienced operators to help test ContestLogX on Linux.
        If you're interested in joining the beta, reach out to
        <a href="mailto:beta@contestlogx.com" class="underline" style="color: #58a6ff;">
          beta@contestlogx.com
        </a>.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
</Layout>
